    "nodes": {
      "41": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "FALLBACK\n\nThe Fallback node will take a signal or event and fall back from that value to the fallback value over its duration. This is only done when the input signal is greater than the current value running in the fallback node. For this reason the Fallback node only works with signals equal or greater than 0.\n\nThe Fallback node is often used to smooth out spiking signals (for example FFT data).\n\nThe Fallback node is related to the Envelope Follower, Smooth, Delay and Curve nodes in that they all follow or smooth out values."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 222.54776000976562,
          "width": 698.1719360351562,
          "x": 334.9342041015625,
